{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1364751-looking-for-a-fitting-chronicle-antagonist","id":1364751,"title":"Looking for a fitting Chronicle Antagonist...","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-02-02T07:30:31-08:00","id":1364751,"title":"Looking for a fitting Chronicle Antagonist...","content":"Ok, so here's the set-up thus far.<br /> <br /> For anyone who saw my previous post, I was humming and hawing over whether to set the chronicle in New Orleans, or my local stomping ground of Exeter and Dartmoor. I ended up going with the latter, mostly because of familiarity for both myself and my wife (who will be the sole player).<br /> <br /> So I've come up with the following as the basic premise for the setting:<ul><li>The Ancient and Royal Freehold of Dumnonia, which covers both Exeter and Dartmoor, is ruled over by the House of Isca - a Lost &quot;family&quot; that adopts Changelings with potential to become future bearers of the Court mantles, thus keeping a strange version of a bloodline going. The House of Isca claims that this tradition dates back to pre-Roman times (hence the name of the Freehold), but there is some level of skepticism that this is actually the case.</li> <li>Until about 50 years ago, Dartmoor was actually its own freehold, but something happened and the Crown of the Seasons was lost. No one is entirely sure what caused this to happen, but in order to keep themselves safe in the towns they had come to call home, the &quot;moorland motleys&quot; (as they became known) accepted the protection and guardianship of the House of Isca, thus becoming an extension of Dumnonia.</li> <li>Dartmoor is actually quite a dangerous place to live - since the collapse of the Moors Freehold, the True Fae (and Hedge denizens) have become emboldened, and have been known to leave the Hedge to hunt and prey on any Lost unfortunate enough to be caught outside the relative safety of the scatter towns and settlements.</li> <li>Lately, resentment has begun to brew among the moorland motleys, who feel that something should be done to reclaim the Crown they lost, and rumours and speculation are growing that somehow, the House of Isca had something to do with the Moor Freehold's tragedy.</li> </ul>The truth of the matter is, the House of Isca <i>did</i> have something to do with the Crown disappearing from the Moor Freehold, although the present bearers of the Isca crown have no knowledge of this. In addition, there is a shadow force that is stirring up the ill feeling in both the moorland motleys and those loyal to Isca, seeking to benefit from the ensuing chaos.<br /> <br /> My question is - who would fit well as this shadowy antagonist that is manipulating everything behind the scenes? It could be a True Fae (or a collective of them working together, which frankly terrifies me), seeking to systematically reclaim the entire Lost population of south Devon when the time is right. It could be a Huntsman playing the long game, destabilising the local political status quo in order to drag one specific quarry back to their Durance. It might be a group of loyalists, hoping to curry favour with their Fae masters by weakening the Lost enough that taking them back to Arcadia becomes much easier.<br /> <br /> I don't have one particularly strong idea on this thus far, however - nothing that leaps out at me and says, &quot;This one! This is the bad guy you're looking for!&quot; As such, any thoughts anyone else has that I could steal - I mean, be inspired by - would be very welcome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25847-jonofdevon","name":"JonOfDevon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-02-02T08:48:56-08:00","id":1364763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JonOfDevon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1364751-looking-for-a-fitting-chronicle-antagonist#post1364751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok, so here's the set-up thus far.<br /> My question is - who would fit well as this shadowy antagonist that is manipulating everything behind the scenes? It could be a True Fae (or a collective of them working together, which frankly terrifies me), seeking to systematically reclaim the entire Lost population of south Devon when the time is right. It could be a Huntsman playing the long game, destabilising the local political status quo in order to drag one specific quarry back to their Durance. It might be a group of loyalists, hoping to curry favour with their Fae masters by weakening the Lost enough that taking them back to Arcadia becomes much easier.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, these all strike me as variations on the most obvious and least interesting answer: that it's all the doing of the Gentry and their catspaws to do the same thing we already know they want to do, just accomplished in a subtler way. That strikes me as kind of unsatisfying: it doesn't reveal any new motive or dimension to the conflict, and it's already clearly suggested by the fact that the catspaws of the Others can be plainly observed to take advantage of the situation.<br /> <br /> The second most obvious answer is that it's a selfish and all-too-human gambit on the part of somebody in or attached to the House of Isca, who doesn't care about feeding Dartmoor to the wolves if it means she can gain more temporal power by presiding over a larger sworn freehold. Or she might have an occult motive that involves having <i>taken</i> the crowns of all four seasons of Dartmoor to harness for some grandiose ambition. This works if your wife wants a game where she will navigate untrustworthy alliances and vendettas within the ironside courts of the Lost, especially if the House of Isca is <i>not</i> complicit as an institution and includes Lost working at cross purposes who would oppose the tyrant if they knew her doings, or are trying to uncover what's happening already. These, in turn, could be a mix of Lost with their own personal agendas and changelings who are without ulterior motive and genuinely believe the patronage and mentorship of the House of Isca can do some legitimate good.<br /> <br /> That brings us to the third level of remove, expanding on that idea of the occult power of seasons above. What if, rather than a story of spies and political intrigue, we're talking about a <i>metaphysical incursion?</i> The mastermind in this case is neither human, nor changeling, nor Gentry, but a <i>court patron</i> on a similar scale to the embodiments of the four seasons themselves with which the Seasonal Courts struck their bargains. The House of Isca in Exeter presumably has their own Bargain to keep from falling to the same fate as Dartmoor, and it might well make sense that this is not the traditional seasonal structure, given the comparatively monolithic sound of the House of Isca and their willingness to transgress against all four seasons at once. What did the House of Isca bargain with to keep Exeter safe from the Wild Hunt? Why is it disrupting a nearby court \u2013 out of jealousy, greed, resentment, scorn, revenge for an old oath broken? How far back into history does this Bargain reach? Did witch-owls follow the Romans into Albion and get snared into a contract of protection by wily Britons?<br /> <br /> The option at the fourth level of remove is, of course, to go to a fully out-of-context problem that does not fit neatly into the metaphysics of Changeling: the Lost. Doppelgangers from the other world reflected through mirrors need to weaken the old Bargains and increase traffic through the Hedge to provide cover for their entry into our world. A strange machine begins to grow through the walls of the city and choke the sewers like kudzu, burning the seasons for fuel. In the absence of the protections of the Seasonal Courts, another Dartmoor is competing for our Dartmoor's place in the world.<br /> <br /> I personally like the third-level option.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}